WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.460
When knitting in pattern we don't want

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.860
to distort the edges of our knitting.

3
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:08.500
By taking time to ensure that the edge

4
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:14.140
needles knit and not pattern,
it will make it easier to seam the pieces

5
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:17.060
and to work in increases and decreases.

6
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.340
For example, when knitting tuck or Slip.

7
00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.780
We don't want the edge
stitches to tuck or slip.

8
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.400
When knitting with a lace carriage,

9
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.780
we certainly don't want the edge
stitch to be an eyelet.

10
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.580
Preventing the edge stitches
from patterning is important.

11
00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.820
Different machines
handle this differently.

12
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.180
Some Brother machines allow you to cancel.

13
00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.380
end needle selection with a setting on

14
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.780
the underside of the carriage.

15
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.540
This is the Brother 260.

16
00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.340
For other Brother machines,
the change knob

17
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.540
also called the KC knob, controls the

18
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.240
selection of the end needles.

19
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.380
With the change knob set to KC1

20
00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:08.000
one, the end needles will automatically
stay in D or middle position.

21
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.660
For example, in this slip stitch pattern,

22
00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.880
the needles that are
in B position will slip.

23
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.020
Here, I've set the change knob to KC 2

24
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.600
and the end needle is being
selected and will slip.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.480
And you.
Can see how this will distort the edge.

26
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.290
Looking at tuck, the same thing applies.

27
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.760
We don't want to tuck
the edge stitch or stitches.

28
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.490
With the edge being plain,

29
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.820
it will  not only be easier to seam
that edge, but when shaping increases or

30
00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:00.520
decreases, you won't be dealing
with a tuck or slip strand.

31
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.020
Because Brother machines
preselect the needles.

32
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:11.580
We also have the opportunity to position
any edge needles so they don't pattern.

33
00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.580
Just position them into
"D" position before you.

34
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:19.610
Knit the row to manually
keep the edges clean.

35
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.300
End needle selection is important when
working in pattern to obtain neat edges

36
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:28.800
for seaming and for making shaping
easier and neater as well.

